    "fileId": 6579,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Synovial sarcoma",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "Graphical representation of interrelationships of fusion type to tumour histology and patient sex in synovial sarcoma. Most synovial sarcomas are monophasic, and the <em>SS18</em>-<em>SSX1</em> fusion type is more common than <em>SS18</em>-<em>SSX2</em>. Men and women are affected in approximately even proportions, but men show a 3:2&nbsp;ratio of <em>SS18</em>-<em>SSX1</em> to <em>SS18</em>-<em>SSX2</em>, whereas in women the ratio is close to 1:1. The vast majority of <em>SS18</em>-<em>SSX2</em> cases show monophasic histology, whereas most biphasic cases show <em>SS18</em>-<em>SSX1</em>. Biphasic synovial sarcomas harbouring the <em>SS18</em>-<em>SSX2</em> fusion are uncommon, and the diagnosis of such cases should therefore be well supported.",
    "source": "Marc Ladanyi",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/33/121/6579.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/33/121/thumb_6579.jpg"
  }
